## Why This Is Asked
Addresses detection/containment of DNS exfiltration in multi-cloud, a gap in the existing questions.

## Key Concepts
- DNS exfiltration patterns (long queries, high entropy)
- Cross‑cloud log sources (AWS Route 53 Resolver logs, IBM DNS, Cloudflare Gateway logs)
- SIEM correlation (Splunk/QRadar)
- Containment actions (Cloudflare Gateway blocking, firewall rules) and IR coordination

## Code Example
```javascript
function dnsExfiltrationDetected(log) {
  const longQuery = log.query.length > 60;
  const highEntropy = /[A-Za-z0-9+/]{60,}/.test(log.query);
  const unusualTLD = /\.(xyz|top|icu|cc)$/i.test(log.domain);
  return longQuery && highEntropy && unusualTLD;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you tune FP/FN rates across clouds?
- How would you test the IR playbook without impacting business?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks ability to design secure, compliant AI deployments across clouds, covering governance, identity, data integrity, and responder readiness.

## Key Concepts
- CISSP domains: Security & Risk Management, IAM, Security Architecture, Security Operations
- Policy-as-Code (OPA/Rego)
- Model provenance, artifact signing, immutable logs
- ABAC, least privilege, cross-cloud auditable logs
- Incident response for model poisoning and supply-chain risk

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: sign artifact (simplified)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signArtifact(data, privKey) {
  const sign = crypto.createSign('SHA256');
  sign.update(data);
  sign.end();
  return sign.sign(privKey, 'hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you integrate policy checks into CI/CD and runtime gates?
- How would you validate provenance and detect poisoned models before serving?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CISSP alignment on secure messaging design: threat modeling, cryptography, device attestation, revocation, and incident response, across multiple platforms.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end encryption and forward secrecy
- Device attestation and binding keys to a trusted device
- Key rotation, revocation, and audit logging
- Threat modeling methods (STRIDE/PASTA)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: rotate per-user keys (simplified)
async function rotateUserKey(userId) {
  const newKey = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({name:'AES-GCM', length:256}, true, ['encrypt','decrypt']);
  await storeKey(userId, newKey);
  await revokeOldKeys(userId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate key compromise containment in a live system?
- What logs and metrics would you collect to support forensics without exposing plaintext data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design privacy-conscious telemetry for a multi-tenant SaaS, balancing observability with isolation and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Data minimization, per-tenant isolation, and role-based access controls
- Edge-to-central telemetry security, encryption in transit/rest
- SBOMs, reproducible builds, code signing, and attestation
- Runtime integrity checks and tabletop incident validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// PII redaction example for telemetry payload
function redact(payload){
  const safe = {...payload};
  if (safe.userEmail) safe.userEmail = 'REDACTED';
  return safe;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-tenant key management and rotation?
- What are the observability vs privacy trade-offs and how would you validate them in practice?
